# Exploration Report: WIP Picker fed by Department-Scoped BOM Components

## 1. `PlanningControlCenterClient.js` (`src/features/production-planning/components/`)

### `wipSkuCatalog` — prop chain & shape
Fetched via SWR at lines 244–258, **not** a prop — the page component fetches it directly:

```js
// line 245-249
const { data: skuCatalogData } = useSWR("/api/production-planning/schedule/sku-catalog", fetcher, ...);
// line 251-258
const wipSkuCatalog = useMemo(() => {
  const items = skuCatalogData?.data || [];
  return items.filter((item) =>
    String(item?.iccat_code || "").trim() === "03" ||
    String(item?.sku_code || "").startsWith("WIP"));
}, [skuCatalogData]);
```

**⚠️ Shape mismatch bug to be aware of:** the API (`src/app/api/production-planning/schedule/sku-catalog/route.js:150-166`) returns **camelCase** entries `{ skuCode, name, brand, unit, hasBom, formulaOptions, itemType }` — but the filter (line 255-256), the dropdown options (line 959-960 `sku.sku_code`), and `handleLineChange` (line 338-341 `sku.sku_code` / `matchSku.sku_name` / `matchSku.uom_name`) all read **snake_case** keys that don't exist in the response. So `iccat_code`/`sku_code` are always `undefined` and auto-fill never matches. Your new picker should use the API's actual camelCase fields (`skuCode`, `name`, `unit`, `itemType === "WIP"`).

### Section model — section → department mapping
`activeSection` (state, line 56) is one entry of `sectionOptions` (line 210), which comes from:

```js
// line 203-206
const sectionShiftKey = activeDeptId
  ? `/api/production-section-shifts?departmentId=${encodeURIComponent(activeDeptId)}` : null;
```

Each section object (built server-side in `src/lib/productionSectionShifts.js:85-99` `buildSectionShiftGroups`, sourced from `src/app/api/production-section-shifts/route.js:43-59`) has:

```js
{ id, name, displayOrder, templateId,
  departmentId, departmentCode, departmentName,   // ← section → department binding
  shifts: [{ shiftId, shiftCode, shiftName, startTime, endTime, ... }] }
```

Yes — a section maps to exactly one production department via `departmentId` (section defs live in `production_schedule_section_defs`, joined to departments through `production_schedule_templates.dept_id`). Note the page already selects the department first (`activeDeptId`, line 47) and fetches sections filtered by it, so `activeSection.departmentId === activeDeptId` in practice.

### `WipEditorModal` data flow & `handleLineChange`
- Defined at line 1296; rendered at line 1264-1276 with props `{ activeSection, sectionOptions, sectionRows, isLocked, wipSkuCatalog, getWipMeta, getShiftsForSection, handleLineChange, handleAddWipRow, handleRemoveWipRow, onClose }`. `sectionRows` = `draftLines` filtered to `activeSection` (lines 1133-1137, matching on `sourceSectionDefId`/`sourceSectionName`). It's a single minified JSX line (1297); the รหัส WIP cell renders a `<select>` whose onChange calls `handleLineChange(row.tempId, "skuCode", value)`.
- `handleLineChange` (lines 330-347): immutable-update of the `draftLines` local state entry by `tempId`; when `field === "skuCode"` it looks up the catalog and auto-fills `skuName` and `unit`:

```js
if (field === "skuCode") {
  const matchSku = wipSkuCatalog.find((sku) => sku.sku_code === value);
  if (matchSku) {
    updated.skuName = matchSku.sku_name || "";
    updated.unit = matchSku.uom_name || matchSku.uom || "kg";
  }
}
```

Draft line shape (from `handleAddWipRow`, lines 361-375): `{ tempId, workDate, skuCode, skuName, plannedQty, unit, shiftId, shiftCode, shiftName, sourceSectionDefId, sourceSectionName, workstreamCode, note }`.

### API routes this page calls
- `GET /api/production-planning/schedule/inbox` (line 62) — master plans
- `GET /api/production-planning/departments?scope=assigned` (line 115) — user's departments
- `GET /api/production-planning/wip-plan/draft?masterPlanNo&departmentId` (line 141); `POST` same (save, line 451)
- `POST /api/production-planning/explosion/preview` (lines 180, 406) — material preview
- `GET /api/production-section-shifts?departmentId=` (line 204) — sections + shifts
- `GET /api/production-planning/schedule/sku-catalog` (line 246) — SKU catalog (the dropdown source)
- `GET /api/production-planning/wip-products?limit=1000` (line 262) — WIP batch-conversion meta (`skuCode, skuName, uomName, wipQtyInputMode, unitsPerBatch, allowMasterPlan, formulaOptions`)
- `POST /api/production-planning/wip-plan/confirmed-subplans` (line 516)

## 2. Department-products binding (`/production-line/settings/department-products`)

- Component: `src/features/production-line-settings/components/DepartmentProductScopeTab.js` (1355 lines; also manages departments and users). It loads assignable products via `fetchProductsCache({ iccatCodes: "03,04,04-1,04-2", ... })` (line 209-214) — i.e. **WIP + FG products**, each a `products_cache` row (`SKU_CODE/sku_code`, name, uom, iccat). Client wrappers in `src/features/production-line-settings/services/productionLineSettingsClient.js:107-126` call `/api/production-department-products` (GET/POST/DELETE).
- API: `src/app/api/production-department-products/route.js`. GET (`departmentId` required) queries:

```js
// line 30-34
.from("production_department_products")
.select("sku_code,is_owner,products_cache(sku_name,iccat_code,iccat_name,uom_name)")
.eq("department_id", departmentId)
```

  Response shape: `{ data: [{ sku_code, is_owner, sku_name, iccat_code, iccat_name, uom_name }] }`.
- Table `production_department_products` (migration `supabase/migrations/20260417_add_production_department_scopes.sql:28-34`): `id bigserial PK, department_id uuid FK→production_departments, sku_code text FK→products_cache(sku_code), is_owner bool (added later), created_at, created_by`; unique `(department_id, sku_code)`. The "product" is a **`products_cache.sku_code`** (may be FG `04*` or WIP `03`; planning scope code consistently treats them via `is_owner = true` as the dept's "owned" SKUs).

## 3. BOM structure & WIP-vs-RAW detection

Tables (base migration `supabase/migrations/20260215_add_production_bom.sql`):
- `production_boms`: `id, sku_code (unique FK→products_cache), status, ...` — one BOM header per parent SKU (FG or WIP).
- `production_bom_versions`: `id, bom_id, version_no, status ('draft'|'review'|'locked'→ later also 'approved'), formula_code, formula_name, base_batch, base_output_boxes, wip_qty_input_mode, deleted_at, ...`
- `production_bom_items` (`src/types/supabase.ts:708-725`): `id, version_id FK, material_sku_code, material_goods_code|null, qty, uom_name|null, calculation_basis, planning_offset_days, dough_set_base, dough_set_round_mode, headcount_base, headcount_round_mode, machine_base, machine_round_mode, notes, created_at`. **There is no item-type column** — WIP vs RAW is derived from the material's `products_cache.iccat_code` via `src/lib/bomMatrix.js`:

```js
// bomMatrix.js:1-3, 23-28
export const FG_CATEGORY_OPTIONS  = ["04", "04-1", "04-2"];
export const WIP_CATEGORY_OPTIONS = ["03"];
export const RAW_CATEGORY_OPTIONS = ["01", "02", "05", "08", "10"];
resolveItemTypeByCategory(code) → "FG" | "WIP" | "RAW" | "UNKNOWN"
```

Existing FG→WIP explosion helpers:
- **DB RPC** `explode_bom_batch` / `explode_bom_batch_with_planning_offsets` wrapped in `src/lib/bomExplosionRpc.js` (recursive, maxDepth 12, returns per-row `{ rootSkuCode, materialSkuCode, materialGoodsCode, materialName, itemType, requiredQty, uomName, planningOffsetDays, ... }`).
- `src/lib/wipPlanning.js:638` `explodeWipDemandFromMainRows(supabaseAdmin, mainRows)` — explodes FG main-plan rows and keeps only `row.itemType === "WIP"` (line 736) → exactly the "WIPs that are BOM components of FGs" logic you need, keyed per department/section/shift.
- `src/app/api/production-planning/wip-products/route.js` does **not** explode BOMs — it lists all `products_cache` rows with `iccat_code = "03"` plus approved-BOM version meta and `production_wip_policies.allow_master_plan` flags.
- `src/app/api/production-planning/schedule/sku-catalog/route.js:54-109` shows the established pattern for "SKU → production_boms → production_bom_versions (status='approved', deleted_at is null, latest version_no per formula_code)".

## 4. Existing department-scope → allowed-FG patterns

Yes, several precedents exist; none currently returns "WIP components of dept-allowed FGs" (you'd add that), but the dept→SKU set part is solved:

- **`src/lib/planningScope.js:13-75` `loadPlanningScopeByUser`** — the canonical helper: user's editor departments from `production_department_users` → SKU set from `production_department_products where department_id in (...) and is_owner = true`. Returns `{ canManageGlobal, editableDepartmentIdSet, editableSkuSet }`.
- Same inline pattern in `src/app/api/production-planning/calculate/route.js:83-96`, `src/app/api/production-planning/schedule/inbox/route.js:77-96` (`ownerSkuSet`), `src/app/api/production-planning/status/route.js:671,752`, `src/app/api/production-planning/inbox/route.js:79`, `src/lib/scale/products.js:102`.
- How the planning page knows a section's department: client-side via the `departmentId` field on each section from `/api/production-section-shifts` (already filtered by `activeDeptId`); server-side via `production_schedule_section_defs.template_id → production_schedule_templates.dept_id` (`src/app/api/production-section-shifts/route.js:10-60`).

**Suggested composition for your new endpoint** (matches existing idioms): dept's allowed FG skus from `production_department_products` (decide whether to use `is_owner = true` like planning scope, or all assigned like the settings tab) → `production_boms` + approved `production_bom_versions` for those FGs → `production_bom_items` of those versions joined to `products_cache` where `iccat_code = '03'` (or reuse `explode_bom_batch` RPC per FG if you want transitive/multi-level WIPs — note direct-item query gives only level-1 components, while the feature description says "components (via BOM)" which the RPC handles recursively).

## 5. Route-handler conventions in `src/app/api/production-planning/**`

Consistent pattern across all planning routes:

```js
import { NextResponse } from "next/server";                    // or plain Response.json
import { getSupabaseAdmin, jsonError } from "@/lib/adminApi";  // service-role client + error helper
import { requirePermissionKeys } from "@/lib/permissionGuard"; // auth; returns { userId, isAdmin, permissions }

export async function GET(request) {
  try {
    await requirePermissionKeys(request, [
      "production-line-planning-view",
      "production-line-planning-manage",
      "plan.edit_own_dept",           // dept-scoped keys commonly included
    ]);
    const db = getSupabaseAdmin();
    // ... db.from(...).select(...) with snake_case columns
    return NextResponse.json({ ok: true, data: ... });   // or { data, meta }
  } catch (error) {
    return jsonError(error);
  }
}
```

Notes:
- Permission keys in use for planning read endpoints: `production-line-planning-view`, `production-line-planning-manage`, `plan.calculate_own_dept`, `plan.edit_own_dept`, `plan.view_all`.
- SKU normalization everywhere: `String(value||"").trim().toUpperCase()` (see `normalizeSku` in `src/lib/wipPlanning.js:12`, `planningScope.js:5`).
- Shared server libs live in `src/lib/` with `import "server-only"` (`wipPlanning.js`, `planningScope.js`, `bomExplosionRpc.js`, `bomMatrix.js`).
- Defensive `isMissingColumnError`/`isMissingTableError` guards (PGRST204 / 42P01) are the house style for optional columns like `allow_master_plan` and `deleted_at`.
